.TH CVECTOR_BEGIN_ITER 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cvector_begin_iter \- create an iterator positioned at a vector's first element
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cmap_iterator *cvector_begin_iter(cvec v, char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates an iterator positioned at element 0. The iterator walks the
vector in index order. The key of the iterator is the zero-based index
of the element. Its value is the element itself. The caller reads both
with the unified accessors ccol_iter_key_ptr(3) and
ccol_iter_val_ptr(3). These accessors give typed pointers straight into
the backing buffer of the vector. A write through the value pointer
therefore updates the element in place.

The generic ccol_begin(3) macro selects this function for a cvec. Call
this function directly only when the container type is already known and
the caller wants the error string. ccol_begin(3) is the ordinary entry
point.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v     Vector to iterate over, or NULL
err   Optional pointer to receive an error string on failure, or NULL
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to a newly allocated iterator positioned at the first
element.
Returns NULL when there is nothing to iterate (v is NULL or the vector
holds no elements), with *err set to NULL.
Returns NULL with *err set to an error string when the iterator itself
could not be allocated.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function sets *err to NULL at entry, before anything else. Test
*err, and not the return value, to tell an empty result apart from a
failure
A NULL v iterates as an empty vector. It does not report an error. This
is a deliberate cross-module contract. chashmap_begin_iter(3) and
cbmap_begin_iter(3) share it. The caller can therefore iterate a lazily
created container field that is still NULL because nothing is in it
yet. No caller needs to write its own NULL guard first
The iterator frees itself when ccol_iter_next(3) steps past the last
element and returns NULL. An iteration that stops early must end with
ccol_iter_destroy(3). The caller can also declare the iterator with
ccol_iter_declare(3), so that scope exit destroys it
The iterator comes from the vector's own memory management procedures
Every operation that inserts, removes or resizes invalidates the
iterator
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_push(vec, 10);
cvec_push(vec, 20);

char *err = NULL;
ccol_iter_declare(vec, it);
for (it = cvector_begin_iter(vec, &err); it; it = ccol_iter_next(it)) {
    printf("[%zu] = %d\en", *ccol_iter_key_ptr(it),
                            *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it));
}
if (err) {
    fprintf(stderr, "cannot iterate: %s\en", err);
}
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_begin (3),
.BR ccol_iter_next (3),
.BR ccol_iter_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3),
.BR chashmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cbmap_begin_iter (3)
